]. It then comes down to the strategy in what to discard from the singles. I can discard B2, since that is isolated. I can use the opportunity to void B to gain a point too.\\n\\nAfter careful consideration of all options, I think my best play is to Peng W7. It sets me up well. It allows me to build a hand with high point potential. It also doesn't rely on drawing lucky tiles.\\n\\nI need to Peng W7. By doing this, I will have an All Pungs strategy with the B7 as a target. This leads to a Mixed Double Pung, and the hand will have 9 points. I need to get B7, F2, and T3 Pungs for this! It's better than Seven Pairs. The current hand has 3 pairs and 1 pung.\\n\\nFinal Answer: Peng W7. The calculations check out and the strategy is strong.\\n\\n\\n\", \"output\": \"Evaluation: The current hand contains three pairs (W7, B7, F2) and one pung (T3), making it 3-shanten for Seven Pairs but effectively 2-shanten for an All Pungs hand. The discard W7 allows forming a Pung of W7 immediately. By calling Peng on W7, the hand structure solidifies towards All Pungs (6 points). Furthermore, holding a pair of B7s creates the opportunity to form a Pung of B7s, which would complete the \\\"Mixed Double Pung\\\" pattern (2 points) with the W7s. Additionally, the F2 pair (South Wind) offers a potential \\\"Pung of Terminals or Honors\\\" (1 point). The combination of All Pungs (6) + Mixed Double Pung (2) + Pung of Honors (1) totals 9 points, satisfying the 8-point minimum. Passing would forgo this opportunity and leave the hand relying on slow self-draws.\\n\\nReason: Calling Peng on W7 is the most efficient path to completing the hand. It transitions the hand towards a high-scoring All Pungs shape and sets up the Mixed Double Pung pattern (W7 and B7), which is crucial for meeting the minimum point requirement.\\n\\nAnswer: Peng W7\"}", "tile": "{\"system_prompt\": \"\\n    You are a Chinese Standard Mahjong (\\u56fd\\u6807\\u9ebb\\u5c06, or MCR Mahjong) player. It is a four-player game, 